ol.li:
Confirming that the evidence id and timestamp are
consistent with the token's iat claim (i.e., the
user confirmation occurred before or at token issuance);

ul.li:
Evidence cannot be moved from one token to another without
detection (the token signature would not cover the new
evidence);

Publish the AS's evidence signing keys at a well-known
JWKS endpoint () accessible to
the RS;

section.t:
A revocation handle grants no authority: it is a lookup key
for a consent record, not a credential.  Nevertheless,
implementations MUST protect against the following threats:

ul.li:
Enumeration: handles MUST contain at least 128 bits of
entropy so that an attacker cannot discover consent records
by guessing handle values;
